before moving on.

00:06:27.350 --> 00:06:30.070
So we tried to group the
deciding factors for your story

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:32.560
solution into three pillars.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:36.730
Functionality, such as data
model, query languages,

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:38.560
partitioning, et cetera.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:41.200
Scalability, which,
in the IoT world,

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:44.020
always will be the
deciding factor.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:46.090
And lastly, cost.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:49.780
Let's see if this
makes sense to.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:53.080
So there are many
types of data models.

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:55.090
Just look at, like,
many books there

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:58.390
are on data normalization,
or stuff like that.

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:00.910
And every data model comes
with assumptions about how

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:03.490
it's going to be used.

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:07.540
Since the data model has such
a profound effect on what

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:10.220
the application can
do, or cannot do,

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:12.790
it's super important to
choose one that is appropriate

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:14.650
for your use case.

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:16.930
A good practice is to
lay out the queries

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:19.210
that you want to
be able to perform

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:21.820
on your data ahead of time.

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:23.380
That will give you
a great insights

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:26.490
in what data model to use.

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:28.990
The relational model
does a very good job

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:31.480
of mind hiding
implementation details

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:33.590
behind a clean interface.

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:40.080
However, the use cases we are
looking at, other data models

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.400
might be a much better fit.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.440
Let me give you a few examples.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:46.690
So we usually need
greater scalability

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:51.240
than what relational databases
easily can achieve, especially

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:53.190
when it comes to
very large data sets

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:58.890
or very high write throughput.

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:01.700
A desire for more
flexible schema design,

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:04.550
or a more expressive data model.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:07.440
For example, schema read
instead of schema write.

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:14.970
So many people feel that a
declarative language, like SQL,

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:17.610
is much easier to work
with than an imperative

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:19.740
one like JavaScript.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:21.270
And as we've
discussed previously,

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:24.330
the relational model,
and subsequently, SQL,

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.270
does a really good job of
hiding implementation details

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:29.470
of the database engine.

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:31.440
So that means that
the database vendor

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.059
can introduce performance
improvements under the hood

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:39.390
without you having to
change any of your queries.

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:42.210
On the other hand, many
non-relational database that

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:44.670
support specialized
query operations

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:47.310
that might fit our use
case much, much better.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:50.355
One example would be
efficient sequential reads.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:56.180
In a distributed
system, first, we

00:08:56.180 --> 00:09:00.380
might just address the
terminal logical confusion,

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:01.880
that this is all
the same things.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:03.950
It's just what
different databases call

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:08.400
charting or partitioning or
regions or tablets and whatnot.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:11.420
But in a distributed
system, data

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:12.942
will always be partitioned.

00:09:12.942 --> 00:09:14.900
So when you have a system
with data partitioned

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:18.200
over several nodes, how will you
go about scaling your cluster

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:21.800
up and down, as more nodes
get added to the cluster?

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:26.040
Will the database help you
reassign data to new nodes,

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:29.250
or is that something that
you'll have to manage yourself?

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:31.070
And if you have to
manage it yourself,

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.200
will that make you
have to compromise

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:35.300
on your scalability goals?

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.000
In some systems,
assigning ownership

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:42.440
of a partition to a new node
is a online metadata operation

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:47.690
that doesn't require
any data motion.

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:50.960
Spoiler alert, I think
Billy will talk about one

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:54.250
of these systems
in a little bit.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:56.880
So, replication.

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:59.070
Replication basically
means that we

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:02.930
are keeping a copy of the
same data on multiple nodes,

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:05.100
on multiple machines.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:06.870
There are several
reasons why you might

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:09.300
like your data replicated.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:12.240
So, for once, you
maybe want to make sure

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:15.630
that your data is graphically
close to your users.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:19.060
Another one could be for
high availability reasons.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:22.710
Maybe you want to increase
your throughput by adding

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:24.360
additional read replicas.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.150
Or maybe you want to do it
for workload separation.

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:29.760
You might have one
cluster serving data,

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:35.820
and an identical cluster for
your analytical workloads.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:37.060
And lastly, cost.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:39.450
So how is the database licence?

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:41.070
Is it open source?

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:42.930
Does the provider charge
you for the amount

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:47.040
of data that your store, or for
the throughput that you get?

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:48.360
Or something else?

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:49.650
Is it a managed service?

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:52.080
Do you need to take on
management and monitoring

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:53.130
of the database?

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:56.110
And if so, how do you
quantify that cost?

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:57.990
What does support cost?

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:00.598
Do you have the competence
for this technology in-house,

00:11:00.598 --> 00:11:02.140
or do you need to
train people on it?

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:06.098
We did it.

00:11:06.098 --> 00:11:07.765
Hopefully, we are on
the same page, now,

00:11:07.765 --> 00:11:09.680
and have a common language.

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:11.680
So if there's one thing
we want you to take away

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:14.770
from this session, it
is don't treat storage

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:16.221
as an afterthought.

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:22.440
So we've identified two
different iota use cases.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:25.800
The first one we call
complex application logic.

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:30.020
This is where the read to
write ratio is balanced.

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:32.720
These applications are not
just about receiving data

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:36.570
from devices, but also
controlling the devices.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.600
So the complex
application logic is

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:41.300
used to determine some
kind of change that needs

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:44.040
to be performed on the device.

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:46.310
The other type of application
that we've identified

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:49.910
is something we called
data ingestion intensive.

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:53.780
Here, we have many devices
sending a lot of data upstream.

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:56.200
These applications are
write intensive, as

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.640
opposed to read intensive.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:03.460
And they are usually used
for monitoring or analytics.

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:07.950
With that said, let's
look at a few scenarios.

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:13.170
The first scenario, we will look
at a newly founded IoT startup.

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:16.250
The IoT startup is still in
the beginning of their journey,

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:17.980
so the team is still small.

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:20.830
They have developed a platform
for smart home appliances

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:25.690
like light bulbs, thermostats,
smoke detectors, et cetera.

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:29.440
The data from each
smart home appliance

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:34.030
is very suitable to be stored
as a self-contained document.

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:36.070
And the smart home
appliances are being

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:37.790
controlled by mobile apps.

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:39.520
So a family, for
example, can have

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:44.830
several mobile apps that are
controlling these appliances.

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:49.020
So let's map these two
to technical features.

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:51.750
Small team and
limited resources.

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:54.960
Most company would probably
benefit from serverless today,

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:59.070
but, for a small startup, it
might be absolutely crucial.

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:01.320
If you have a small
team of developers,

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:03.270
letting them focus
on your core business

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:08.840
instead of managing servers
could make all the difference.

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:11.180
Self-contained documents
sounds like something

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:15.560
that would be a good fit
for a document database.

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:20.038
The hierarchical structure lends
itself to good data locality.

00:13:20.038 --> 00:13:21.830
And the fact that there
is mobile apps that

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:24.020
are being used to
control the appliances

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:29.540
gives us a hint that this is a
complex application logic case.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:32.660
So this would be closer to
the first of the two use

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:34.070
cases we discussed earlier.

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:42.390
So this startup, they don't
only face the challenges of IoT,

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:44.790
they also have to
manage mobile apps.

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:46.560
So let's have a
quick look what it

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:50.940
would take to build a mobile
database the hard way.

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:52.410
First, you need a
database service

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:55.750
like MySQL, Postgres or Mongo.

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:59.100
And you've got to manage and
scale that database yourself.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:01.510
Then you want to query
the database from afar.

00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:03.430
So you need an API layer.

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:06.690
And that API layer needs
to be able to authenticate

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:09.260
and authorize your queries.

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:11.700
And finally, you need
to keep state locally.

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:14.100
And that requires
yet, another database.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:18.350
And that database has to be kept
in sync with the remote one.

00:14:18.350 --> 00:14:20.310
So that's a lot of stuff to do.

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:23.220
And that's only to
fetch some simple data.

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:26.240
And I mean, let's
not even get started

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:28.400
on what it would
take to make that

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:31.610
a real time system, instead
of a [INAUDIBLE] one.

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:35.790
Let's see how we
can simplify this.

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:38.070
Enter Firestore.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:41.992
How many in here are
familiar with Firestone?

00:14:41.992 --> 00:14:42.948
That's pretty good.

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:50.680
So Firestore is what we
call a serverless database.

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:55.300
So serverless doesn't mean
that there are no servers, what

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:59.290
it means is that everything from
capacity planning, upgrades,

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:03.490
maintenance, all of that
is handled seamlessly

00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:06.700
by the system without
scheduled downtime.

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:09.910
Multi-region replication, we
keep your app and user data

00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:13.750
safe and available
across regions--

00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:15.250
in case of disaster--

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:18.580
without any configuration
on your side.

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:22.030
Firestore is a globally
distributed database that

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:23.650
will scale based on your usage.

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:29.990
Cloud Firestore lets
us structure our data

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:31.760
as collection and
documents, which

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:33.990
makes queries much easier.

00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:38.170
We can store related data
together in hierarchies.

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:41.440
We based this model on what we
learned from Firebase Realtime

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:42.850
Database developers.

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:45.430
We studied how people
wanted to model their data,

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:48.370
supporting the most common
use cases with patents

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:52.300
that most developers
designed intuitively.

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:55.420
So far, we noticed that this
reduces the learning curve,

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:59.723
and that the data model
is designed to scale.

00:15:59.723 --> 00:16:01.140
Let's look at
Firestore in action.

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:09.540
So can we switch to
the demo, please?

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:12.260
This is actually super
good, this is a live demo.

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:14.910
But we have the creator of
the demo in the audience.

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:20.650
So if stuff goes
sideways, you know?

00:16:20.650 --> 00:16:28.870
So we have four quadrants here,
forming the Firebase logo.

00:16:28.870 --> 00:16:35.340
And then we have 40,000
clients that we have simulated.

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:42.260
And 10,000 clients are assigned
to each of the quadrants.

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:45.910
So what we do here, is that
all these 10,000 clients,

00:16:45.910 --> 00:16:49.140
they are listening to
one of these inputs.

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:54.600
So, of course, no
one will get upset.

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:56.590
We do zero based indexing here.

00:16:56.590 --> 00:16:57.360
So this is zero.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:16:57.930
This is one.

00:16:57.930 --> 00:16:58.440
This is two.

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.240
And it is three.

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:04.050
So 10,000 is listening to
each of these four documents.

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:06.690
When they see a
change, they will then

00:17:06.690 --> 00:17:10.470
write that change back
into this collection,

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:13.109
which is called pixels.

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:16.890
And these are then 40,000
different documents.

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:22.680
So, when we do a change on one
of the documents over here,

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:33.540
that will trigger 10,000 reads
from one document, and 10,000

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:36.150
writes to different documents.

00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:40.270
And, hopefully, it will also
change the color of this.

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:43.080
So, if we take this one
and we change the color,

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:44.250
like this, to red--

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:50.711
and now it should--

00:17:56.680 --> 00:17:58.340
there you go.

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:04.240
So we can change
it back like this.

00:18:07.960 --> 00:18:11.890
And, if you think
this is fake, then we

00:18:11.890 --> 00:18:15.850
can actually look at the
different pixels here.

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:18.460
So let's switch it
back to red again.

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:23.350
And now, you have to be focused
because it will just flicker.

00:18:31.458 --> 00:18:32.000
There you go.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:33.520
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:34.020
Thank you.

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:34.380
Thank you.

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:34.880
Thank you.

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:42.610
We can go back to
the presentation.

00:18:42.610 --> 00:18:43.110
Thank you.

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:50.850
All right, so this
sounds amazing, right?

00:18:50.850 --> 00:18:52.530
Firestore.

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:54.950
So why would anyone
choose another database

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:57.690
over Firestore?

00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:00.360
Maybe Billy can
help us answer that.

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:02.660
BILLY JACOBSON: All
right, thanks, Daniel.

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:05.620
Glad it worked.

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:09.510
So yeah, all this
does look really good.

00:19:09.510 --> 00:19:12.420
So that's kind of the first
case, where it's complex logic.

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:14.790
But if you're maybe more
in the data ingestion

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:19.620
intensive examples of
a use case for IoT,

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:21.395
you might need a
different solution.

00:19:21.395 --> 00:19:22.770
So I've got a few
examples for us

00:19:22.770 --> 00:19:26.100
look at that have
massive QPS requirements,

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:28.290
and solutions that are
going to work for them.

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:29.832
The first are going
to take a look at

00:19:29.832 --> 00:19:33.360
is for Fleet
Monitoring, using IoT.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:36.000
So let's say we are an
enterprise scale company that

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.340
wants to put some kind
of monitoring device

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:41.310
in a fleet of cars, or trucks.

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:44.470
We'd like to have a dashboard to
monitor our fleet in real time,

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:48.390
so we could detect anomalies
and provide feedback

00:19:48.390 --> 00:19:49.710
to the drivers.

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:52.365
We'd also like to do the same
with the historical data.

00:19:52.365 --> 00:19:54.240
Store that in the database
and potentially do

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:55.950
machine learning on it.

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:58.950
And maybe use that to optimize
the fleet, or use that

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:01.390
to train our data.

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:05.980
So if we translate these needs
into the business [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:08.020
the technical
requirements, we need

00:20:08.020 --> 00:20:12.640
the horizontal scalability,
massive scale, low latency,

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:13.600
and high throughput.

00:20:13.600 --> 00:20:17.230
And this is a perfect
fit for Cloud Bigtable.

00:20:17.230 --> 00:20:19.840
Our massively scalable,
high throughput,

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:22.330
globally distributed,
NoSQL database service.

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:27.090
And this is the
same technology that

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:30.930
powers a range of billion
user Google services.

00:20:30.930 --> 00:20:35.003
We've proven this to work
in search, maps, and Gmail.

00:20:35.003 --> 00:20:37.170
And these are applications
that are getting millions

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:39.370
of database access per second.

00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:42.830
And it's all happening
with low latency.

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:46.490
And if you're thinking a million
QPS is a slow day for you,

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:48.290
then you can just
increase the performance

00:20:48.290 --> 00:20:51.290
by adding more Cloud
Bigtable nodes.

00:20:51.290 --> 00:20:53.940
Throughput will scale linearly
based on the number of nodes.

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:57.170
So 30,000 QPS for three
nodes, or three million

00:20:57.170 --> 00:20:59.870
QPS or 300 nodes.

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:01.880
Based on your performance
and cost requirements,

00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:04.460
you can easily scale
with no downtime.

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:06.350
You don't have to
make a sacrifice

00:21:06.350 --> 00:21:08.750
for consistent
throughput or latency

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:12.390
as your data size grows.

00:21:12.390 --> 00:21:15.830
So we're going to take a
look at two examples, here.

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:17.810
It's examples of
Fleet Monitoring.

00:21:17.810 --> 00:21:20.720
I've had a chance to work with
that, I think are pretty cool.

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:23.570
The first, were are going to
use the standard Bigtable API.

00:21:23.570 --> 00:21:28.130
And then other, we'll use
the open source GeoMesa API.

00:21:28.130 --> 00:21:32.660
So Cloud Bigtable provides a
ton of raw speed and scale,

00:21:32.660 --> 00:21:34.670
but does it come with a trade?

00:21:34.670 --> 00:21:36.990
This is a NoSQL solution.

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:39.860
And with that, there's a
very minimal query interface.

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:44.030
You can do row level
look-ups or row range scans.

00:21:44.030 --> 00:21:46.550
So you must create a row key
that's going to group data

00:21:46.550 --> 00:21:49.820
together based on the kinds
of queries you want to run.

00:21:49.820 --> 00:21:52.640
For this example, we're looking
at monitoring the New York City

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:53.750
bus system.

00:21:53.750 --> 00:21:57.728
And we want to focus on
individual bus lines over time.

00:21:57.728 --> 00:21:59.270
So we're going to
create a key that's

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:02.870
a concatenation of the bus
line, the timestamp rounded down

00:22:02.870 --> 00:22:05.420
to the hour, and
then the vehicle ID.

00:22:05.420 --> 00:22:07.688
And for every data
point within that hour,

00:22:07.688 --> 00:22:09.980
we can store them in the same
row using BigQuery tables

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:12.900
versioning feature.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:15.860
So, with that, when
we perform a get row,

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:17.360
we're getting it
all the information

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:18.620
for a vehicle over the hour.

00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:22.480
And you can see it kind of
going across Central Park.

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:26.280
But more commonly, we'd
probably use a scan.

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:30.350
And here, we're getting the
entire data set for this M86

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:32.883
select service bus line.

00:22:32.883 --> 00:22:35.300
I'm looking at the heat map,
we already get a bit of data.

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:37.508
We can see where the starts
and stops of the bus are.

00:22:37.508 --> 00:22:39.810
And maybe, if there
any slow points.

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:41.840
But if we want to get
even more information,

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:43.310
we could just append.

00:22:43.310 --> 00:22:46.760
We could change our query
to have the bus line,

00:22:46.760 --> 00:22:49.640
and maybe the timestamp
rounded up to the month.

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:52.590
And then see if that
changes over time.

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:56.390
We've have a code lab
with all this data

00:22:56.390 --> 00:22:57.960
that works at the Bigtable API.

00:22:57.960 --> 00:22:59.120
And we'll have a link
for that at the end,

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:01.412
if you want to play around
with this a little bit more.

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:07.010
So, now, we've seen
the Cloud Bigtable API.

00:23:07.010 --> 00:23:11.280
But Bigtable also has Apache
HBase API compatibility.

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:13.430
So you get to use
that API if you're

00:23:13.430 --> 00:23:15.050
more familiar with each base.

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:17.270
And more importantly,
this opens up

00:23:17.270 --> 00:23:21.080
a ton of additional capabilities
for streaming, time series

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:25.250
analysis, and
geospatial analysis.

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:27.460
For some of these,
the additional APIs

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:29.810
will set up this
row key based schema

00:23:29.810 --> 00:23:32.930
in a way that will maximize
Cloud Bigtable table's speed,

00:23:32.930 --> 00:23:34.990
so you don't have to do that.

00:23:34.990 --> 00:23:37.010
So, we're going to
take a look at GeoMesa.

00:23:37.010 --> 00:23:40.280
And this creates a row key
using the latitude, longitude,

00:23:40.280 --> 00:23:45.570
and timestamp to allow for
performance geospatial queries.

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:47.820
To use it, we change
our Dataflow job

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:50.785
to write features
using the Gmail API.

00:23:50.785 --> 00:23:53.160
And then we're able to perform
more sophisticated queries

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:54.070
easily.

00:23:54.070 --> 00:23:57.420
Here, I've got a query that's
selecting a bounding box

00:23:57.420 --> 00:23:59.640
on a map and a time frame.

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:02.280
And I'm able to do this with
just a few lines of code.

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:07.790
So Daniel and I
worked with this team

00:24:07.790 --> 00:24:10.850
at Google that would put air
quality sensors in the back

00:24:10.850 --> 00:24:12.560
of Google Street View cars.

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:15.260
And we were able to stream their
data through our standard IoT

00:24:15.260 --> 00:24:17.720
pipeline, and set up our
Dataflow job to write

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:20.490
to Bigtable using the mesa API.

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:22.255
We were able to create
a speedy dashboard

00:24:22.255 --> 00:24:24.380
to view the cars as they
drove around San Francisco

00:24:24.380 --> 00:24:25.640
in real time.

00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:30.720
And batch process in three
years of historical data.

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:33.452
So we're seeing some of
that historical data now.

00:24:33.452 --> 00:24:34.910
And these are just
some of the ways

00:24:34.910 --> 00:24:37.790
you can use Cloud Bigtable
as part of your IoT pipeline.

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:43.750
Now, we're going to take a
look at one more IoT scenario,

00:24:43.750 --> 00:24:47.750
and a database to
help out with it.

00:24:47.750 --> 00:24:51.470
Here, we've got another large
scale organization that wants

00:24:51.470 --> 00:24:53.210
to create public transit cards.

00:24:53.210 --> 00:24:56.030
So users can tap their card
on the transit entrance,

00:24:56.030 --> 00:24:58.760
which will remove money from
their card and let them enter.

00:24:58.760 --> 00:25:00.135
And then if they
run out, they'll

00:25:00.135 --> 00:25:02.790
have to go and refill that.

00:25:02.790 --> 00:25:06.120
So this is a combination
of these IoT use cases.

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:08.260
We've got complex logic in here.

00:25:08.260 --> 00:25:12.750
And this is also very
ingestion intensive.

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:16.440
So for the technical
requirements for this,

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:18.630
we need a horizontally
scalable system

00:25:18.630 --> 00:25:21.930
to handle this massive scale
of a public transit system.

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:23.690
We need high
availability, so no is

00:25:23.690 --> 00:25:26.190
going to have an issue
trying to get to their train.

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:29.730
And most importantly, we
need strong consistency,

00:25:29.730 --> 00:25:33.840
since we're dealing with
monetary transactions.

00:25:33.840 --> 00:25:35.550
And Google's offering
of Cloud Spanner

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:37.290
is a great fit for these.

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:40.320
It's the first horizontally
scalable, strongly consistent,

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:41.610
relational database service.

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:48.540
Daniel spoke a bit earlier
about consistency, availability,

00:25:48.540 --> 00:25:51.180
partition tolerance,
as some of the things

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:54.100
you might have to choose
between when picking a database.

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:56.630
And with those databases,
you can only have two.

00:25:56.630 --> 00:25:58.710
But Cloud Spanner
uses special hardware

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:01.020
to allow for all three.

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:04.320
And Cloud Spanner scales
similarly per node

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:07.485
like Cloud Bigtable, however,
to support transactions

00:26:07.485 --> 00:26:09.660
with strong
consistency, writes are

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:12.600
going to be more computationally
expensive than writes

00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:15.600
without strong consistency.

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:19.790
Since Bigtable doesn't
have strong consistency

00:26:19.790 --> 00:26:21.340
amongst rows.

00:26:21.340 --> 00:26:24.570
So Cloud Spanner can provide
up to 2,000 QPS of writes,

00:26:24.570 --> 00:26:28.020
whereas Bigtable can provide
up to 10,000 QPS per writes.

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:30.600
But really, strong
consistency is

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:32.310
why that might be
a deciding factor

00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:34.215
of using Spanner over Bigtable.

00:26:37.175 --> 00:26:39.300
When you do decide to go
with Cloud Spanner though,

00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:41.610
you get the benefits
of a relational and non

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:43.050
relational database.

00:26:43.050 --> 00:26:45.590
On the relational side,
you get structure, schema,

00:26:45.590 --> 00:26:47.100
and enforced data types.

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:49.710
As well as asset transactions
across rows, regions,

00:26:49.710 --> 00:26:51.120
and continents.

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:52.860
And on the
non-relational side, you

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:56.690
get the horizontal scalability
of a NoSQL database.

00:26:56.690 --> 00:26:59.190
If you're used to using a single
node solution with a master

00:26:59.190 --> 00:27:01.740
replica setup, when that
system needs a software

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:04.860
update, security patch, or
any kind of maintenance,

00:27:04.860 --> 00:27:07.410
you may have to take that
system down and have your user's

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:08.280
experience downtime.

00:27:10.920 --> 00:27:12.420
We know your users
and your services

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:15.070
can have scheduled or
unscheduled downtime.

00:27:15.070 --> 00:27:17.130
So because Spanner
is distributed,

00:27:17.130 --> 00:27:19.980
we can move your data around
to perform routine maintenance

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:22.500
under the hood without
any visible changes to you

00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:23.285
or your users.

00:27:25.595 --> 00:27:26.970
Let's take a look
at how we might

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:29.670
use Spanner for this
public transit example.

00:27:29.670 --> 00:27:32.480
We'll define a table
with using schema.

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:35.230
That should be pretty
familiar to any single users.

00:27:35.230 --> 00:27:38.830
And then we can easily update
the schema with no downtime.

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:41.440
Here, we're creating a table
of commuters and their card

00:27:41.440 --> 00:27:41.940
balances.

00:27:44.890 --> 00:27:47.180
When we want to perform
a transaction on this,

00:27:47.180 --> 00:27:49.570
we can first get the balance.

00:27:49.570 --> 00:27:52.450
Then check it meets the
condition of being greater

00:27:52.450 --> 00:27:55.187
than the cost of the fare,
and then deduct the cost

00:27:55.187 --> 00:27:56.395
of the fare from the balance.

00:28:00.410 --> 00:28:02.880
We've now taken a look
at three IoT scenarios.

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:04.950
And found databases
with particular strains

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:07.625
that complement the needs
of these applications.

00:28:07.625 --> 00:28:09.000
But these examples
could generate

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:10.810
massive amounts of data.

00:28:10.810 --> 00:28:12.900
So how do you perform
analytics across these ever

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:14.350
increasing data sets?

00:28:14.350 --> 00:28:16.983
And Daniels got the
answer for that.

00:28:16.983 --> 00:28:18.150
DANIEL BERGQVIST: Thank you.

00:28:18.150 --> 00:28:23.640
So we usually divide databases
into OLTP or OLAP systems.

00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:27.180
Analytical queries
usually need to scan over

00:28:27.180 --> 00:28:31.320
huge number of records, and
calculate aggregate statistics.

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:34.250
Whereas transactional
queries usually

00:28:34.250 --> 00:28:38.130
touch as a small number
of records fetched by key.

00:28:38.130 --> 00:28:41.510
Our go to solution for ad
hoc analytical workloads

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:43.050
is BigQuery.

00:28:43.050 --> 00:28:46.875
So anyone in here
familiar with BigQuery?

00:28:46.875 --> 00:28:48.180
Cool.

00:28:48.180 --> 00:28:51.030
So before we do
anything else, let's

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:53.400
do a super quick BigQuery demo.

00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:55.530
Can we switch back the--

00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:57.930
thank you.

00:28:57.930 --> 00:29:02.130
So here, we have one
of the public data sets

00:29:02.130 --> 00:29:03.810
that are published to BigQuery.

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:08.310
It's all the taxi
trips in New York--

00:29:08.310 --> 00:29:10.980
well, there's several years--
but this particular year

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:12.930
is 2015.

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:16.770
And here, you can see the
different fields that we have,

00:29:16.770 --> 00:29:18.280
and their data types.

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:22.320
So let's say I'm a data
scientist at the city of New

00:29:22.320 --> 00:29:24.450
York, and I want
to just calculate

00:29:24.450 --> 00:29:28.110
the number of taxi trips
per month during this year.

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:35.170
So this is how I would do that.

00:29:35.170 --> 00:29:42.076
I'll use a timestamp
function that will,

00:29:42.076 --> 00:29:45.220
I mean, it will focus
in with granularity.

00:29:45.220 --> 00:29:48.080
This is the timestamp,
and it's the granularity.

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:51.470
So we want to pick month
from the timestamp.

00:29:51.470 --> 00:29:53.470
And then we just count
the number of trips.

00:29:53.470 --> 00:29:57.320
And then we group it by month.

00:29:57.320 --> 00:29:59.410
So let's run this quick query.

00:29:59.410 --> 00:30:02.770
This is not a very BigQuery,
it's probably, like,

00:30:02.770 --> 00:30:07.260
around 150 million records.

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:10.680
And here, you see that you get
it's roughly around 12 million,

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:16.080
13 million taxi trips per month.

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:18.950
And if you want to visualize
this, it's super simple--

00:30:18.950 --> 00:30:20.420
everything is a click away--

00:30:20.420 --> 00:30:25.040
by just importing the
data into Data Studio.

00:30:25.040 --> 00:30:28.510
So can we go back
to the presentation?

00:30:28.510 --> 00:30:30.550
Thanks.

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:31.880
So BigQuery's serverless.

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:36.280
The underlying infrastructure
is being seamlessly managed

00:30:36.280 --> 00:30:37.090
for you.

00:30:37.090 --> 00:30:39.100
You just upload your
data to BigQuery,

00:30:39.100 --> 00:30:40.870
or stream it into BigQuery.

00:30:40.870 --> 00:30:45.500
And BigQuery them provides you
with a standards compliance SQL

00:30:45.500 --> 00:30:47.620
interface, and off you go.

00:30:47.620 --> 00:30:49.450
It also comes with
built in support

00:30:49.450 --> 00:30:54.040
for geospatial and machine
learning capabilities.

00:30:54.040 --> 00:30:58.840
Even those capabilities
are expressed through SQL.

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:02.450
BigQuery also has a
new feature called

00:31:02.450 --> 00:31:06.530
BI Engine, which is a fast,
in memory analysis service.

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:08.750
Traditionally, when
you use BigQuery,

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:12.050
you get stable response times
between three to five seconds,

00:31:12.050 --> 00:31:21.300
regardless, almost, of much
data your query is processing.

00:31:21.300 --> 00:31:22.880
So, three to five
seconds, that's

00:31:22.880 --> 00:31:25.040
great for a data scientist.

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:28.290
But for a dashboard, it
could be a little bit much.

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:31.550
So for the dashboard
we made might

00:31:31.550 --> 00:31:34.070
want to have some
second latency,

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:36.050
and that's what BI
engine gives us.

00:31:39.100 --> 00:31:40.850
BILLY JACOBSON: All
right, thanks, Daniel.

00:31:40.850 --> 00:31:44.390
So, today, our intention isn't
to give you all the answers.

00:31:44.390 --> 00:31:47.990
We want to empower you to know
how to choose a storage system.

00:31:47.990 --> 00:31:51.500
So we looked at complex logic
applications, data ingestion

00:31:51.500 --> 00:31:54.110
intensive applications,
and Daniel just

00:31:54.110 --> 00:32:00.390
showed us how to perform We
see analytics on all of those.

00:32:00.390 --> 00:32:01.940
So take this
knowledge and use it

00:32:01.940 --> 00:32:03.470
to make an informed decision.

00:32:03.470 --> 00:32:05.660
Don't treat storage
as an afterthought.

00:32:05.660 --> 00:32:06.618
Thank you.

00:32:06.618 --> 00:32:08.530
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:08.530 --> 00:32:10.910
DANIEL BERGQVIST: Billy
and I will be around,

00:32:10.910 --> 00:32:13.150
if you want to chat
with us about this,

00:32:13.150 --> 00:32:14.865
or any other storage
related topics.

00:32:14.865 --> 00:32:15.740
BILLY JACOBSON: Yeah.

00:32:15.740 --> 00:32:16.782
DANIEL BERGQVIST: Thanks.

